/*!
    \qmltype Speech
    \inqmlmodule Clayground.Character3D
    \brief Voice output with approximate lip-sync for characters.

    Speech lets a character say things while its mouth moves along.
    Two kinds of input are supported:

    \list
    \li Plain text - spoken via text-to-speech when the platform provides
        an engine (Qt TextToSpeech); the mouth follows a viseme timeline
        derived from the text's letters. Without a TTS engine the same
        timeline animates silently.
    \li Audio files (wav/mp3) - played back while the mouth follows the
        recording. How closely it is read is set by \l accuracy: loudness
        alone, formant bands, or - given the transcript - the script's own
        shapes on the recording's clock.
    \endlist

    The mouth shape is published through the continuous properties
    \l mouthOpen, \l mouthWide and \l mouthRound which \l Head consumes
    via its \c speechSource property. \l Character wires this up
    automatically - typically you only call \l {Character::say}{say()}:

    \qml
    import Clayground.Character3D

    Character {
        id: hero
        Component.onCompleted: hero.say("Hello! Welcome to Clayground.")
    }

    // Or play a recorded line:
    // hero.say("dialog/intro.wav")

    // Advanced configuration via the speech engine:
    // hero.speech.rate = 0.2
    // hero.speech.volume = 0.8
    \endqml

    \sa Character, Head

    \qmlproperty bool Speech::speaking
    \readonly
    \brief True while text or audio speech is in progress.

    \qmlproperty real Speech::mouthOpen
    \readonly
    \brief Current jaw/mouth openness (0-1), smoothed at ~60 Hz.

    \qmlproperty real Speech::mouthWide
    \readonly
    \brief Current sideways mouth stretch (0-1).

    \qmlproperty real Speech::mouthRound
    \readonly
    \brief Current mouth rounding/pucker (0-1).

    \qmlproperty string Speech::currentWord
    \readonly
    \brief The word currently being spoken (only with TTS engines that
    report word progress; empty otherwise).

    \qmlproperty real Speech::volume
    \brief Output volume for TTS and audio playback (0-1).

    \qmlproperty real Speech::rate
    \brief Speaking rate from slow (-1) over normal (0) to fast (1).
    Forwarded to the TTS engine and used to pace silent text timelines.

    \qmlproperty real Speech::pitch
    \brief TTS voice pitch from low (-1) to high (1). No effect on audio files.

    \qmlproperty bool Speech::ttsAvailable
    \readonly
    \brief True when a text-to-speech engine is available at runtime.

    \qmlproperty int Speech::durationMs
    \readonly
    \brief How long the line currently being said lasts.

    Anything scheduling around a line needs this number, and the engine has
    always known it. See also \l {Speech::estimateDurationMs}{estimateDurationMs()},
    which answers the same question without saying anything.

    \qmlproperty enumeration Speech::accuracy
    \brief How hard the audio path works at reading a mouth out of a recording.

    \value Speech.Envelope Loudness and zero crossings. Says how far the jaw
           dropped and nothing about the shape of the mouth.
    \value Speech.Spectral Formant bands: an open vowel opens further than a
           closed one, a front vowel spreads, a back vowel rounds, a fricative
           narrows. The default.
    \value Speech.Aligned The script's own shapes on the recording's clock.
           Needs a transcript - see \l {Speech::sayAudio}{sayAudio()} - and
           falls back to Spectral without one.

    The tiers are not a performance dial: the analysis is a few hundred
    thousand flops for a whole line, once, before playback starts. What
    separates them is time-to-first-sound, the samples held while the analysis
    runs, and - for Aligned - whether anyone wrote the line down.

    No tier leaves the mouth dead. A recording the analyser cannot read falls
    back to the one below on its own, and Envelope is the floor.

    \sa effectiveAccuracy, Character::speechAccuracy

    \qmlproperty enumeration Speech::effectiveAccuracy
    \readonly
    \brief What the last line actually got.

    Only the same as \l accuracy when the audio could carry it. Asking for
    Aligned and reading Spectral back is a normal outcome - an unreadable
    recording or a missing transcript - and not an error.

    \qmlmethod void Speech::say(string what, string transcript)
    \brief Says text or plays an audio file, auto-detected from the string.

    Strings ending in a known audio extension (wav/mp3/ogg/m4a/flac) are
    treated as file paths or URLs, anything else as text to speak. The
    optional \a transcript is forwarded to \l {Speech::sayAudio}{sayAudio()}
    and ignored by the text path.

    \qmlmethod void Speech::sayText(string text)
    \brief Speaks the given text (TTS when available, silent lip-sync otherwise).

    \qmlmethod void Speech::sayAudio(url source, string transcript)
    \brief Plays the given audio file with lip-sync from audio analysis.

    The optional \a transcript is what the recording was recorded saying.
    Given one, and with \l accuracy at \c Speech.Aligned, the mouth takes
    its shapes from that script and only its timing from the audio - which is
    the only way an /m/ closes reliably, since a bilabial is voiced and every
    acoustic measurement of one says "loud" rather than "shut".

    A transcript that does not match the recording is refused rather than
    used, and the tier below takes the line.

    \qmlmethod int Speech::estimateDurationMs(string text)
    \brief How long this engine would take over \a text, without saying it.

    The same number the mouth is driven by, so anything scheduling around a
    line stops having to guess a speech rate of its own.

    \qmlmethod list Speech::wordMarks()
    \brief The current line's words as \c {{offset, ms}} pairs.

    The character offset in the source text and when that word starts. Empty
    before the first line. Available for a recorded line too when it was read
    at \c Speech.Aligned, since the alignment carries them across.

    \qmlmethod void Speech::stop()
    \brief Interrupts the current speech output.

    \qmlsignal Speech::started()
    \brief Emitted when speech output begins.

    \qmlsignal Speech::finished()
    \brief Emitted when speech output ends (also after stop()).
*/
